    "body": "### Problem\n\nWhen opening a directory fails in the new dir browser (for example no read permission):\n\n1. The listing state is discarded, so I end up in an empty *modifiable* `buftype=''` buffer named after the directory. The listing I navigated from is replaced.\n2. Because `b:nvim_dir` was cleared, the BufEnter autocmd retries the failing open on *every* re-entry of that buffer (`<C-^>`, `:b`, ...), repeating the error notification forever.\n\nCause: `runtime/lua/nvim/dir.lua` `load()` calls `close_listing()` when the first render fails; `should_open()` in `runtime/plugin/dir.lua` then passes again on the next BufEnter.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```\nmkdir -p /tmp/x/locked && chmod 000 /tmp/x/locked\nnvim --clean /tmp/x\n\" cursor on locked/, press <CR>  -> EACCES error (expected)\n:e /tmp/x/foo\n<C-^>                            \" -> EACCES error again, on every re-entry\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nError is reported once, and the buffer should not end up as a modifiable non-listing buffer.\n\nOpen design question: what should reentering the failed buffer do?\n\n- a) stay quiet, keep an empty readonly listing; retry only on `R` / `:edit` (matches `:e` on an unreadable file, and the existing reload-failure behavior which keeps a stale listing)\n- b) retry the listing on every re-entry, so it self-heals when the directory becomes readable, and the error repeats while it is not. More accurate, but re-runs the provider on every BufEnter (a concern for future slow providers like ssh) and needs a failed-state marker.\n\n**I have a fix ready for (a) and will open a PR. Happy to try rework to (b) if that is preferred.**\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-a3eff66e31\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nN/A\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nLinux 6.18.40-2-lts\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nkitty\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nbuild from repo\n",
